# Automatic Table of Contents in LaTeX

To generate a table of contents automatically in LaTeX, you simply use the `\tableofcontents` command, which collects all sectioning commands (`\chapter`, `\section`, `\subsection`) and creates a formatted TOC. Here's a complete example:

```latex
\documentclass{book}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage{blindtext} % For generating placeholder text

\begin{document}

\frontmatter
\title{Example Document}
\author{John Doe}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

\chapter{Preface}
\blindtext

\mainmatter
\tableofcontents % This generates the table of contents

\chapter{First Chapter}
\section{Section 1.1}
\blindtext % Generates placeholder text
\subsection{Subsection 1.1.1}
\blindtext

\section{Section 1.2}
\blindtext

\chapter{Second Chapter}
\section{Section 2.1}
\blindtext

\appendix
\chapter{Appendix A}
\section{Section A.1}
\blindtext

\end{document}
```

## Key Commands Explained

The primary command `\tableofcontents` automatically generates the TOC by reading information stored in auxiliary files (.toc, .lof, .lot). The sectioning commands (`\chapter`, `\section`, `\subsection`) automatically add entries to the TOC when processed.

LaTeX compiles the TOC over multiple passes: the first pass writes section information to an auxiliary file, the second pass reads that file to generate the TOC, and subsequent passes update it with any changes. This multi-pass compilation ensures accurate page numbers and hierarchical structure.

